            <ArticleTitle>Predictors of low back pain in a longitudinal study of Iranian nurses and office workers.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>239-44</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3233/WOR-141850</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">A previous survey had indicated high rates of low back pain (LBP) in Iranian nurses and office workers.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To explore possible risk factors, we carried out a longitudinal study of the same subjects.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Baseline information about risk factors and recent history of LBP was collected by self-administered questionnaire from 246 nurses and 182 office workers. Approximately 12 months later, 385 (90%) answered a second questionnaire about LBP in the past month. Predictors of LBP at follow-up were assessed by Poisson regression and summarised by prevalence rate ratios (PRRs) with 95% confidence intervals (CIs).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In a regression model that included all risk factors, the strongest predictor of LBP at follow-up was report of recent LBP at baseline. In addition, LBP was associated with older age, adverse beliefs about the work-relatedness of LBP (PRR 1.3, 95%CI 1.0-1.5), and incentives from piecework or bonuses (PRR 1.4, 95%CI 1.1-1.6). When baseline report of LBP was omitted from the model, associations were also observed with tendency to somatise, poor mental health and time pressures at work.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our findings support the importance of psychosocial risk factors for LBP, including adverse health beliefs and working conditions that encourage higher output.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
